[test-suite name=prof ring=1
  [doc [#
conformance fixtures for cx-stdlib/prof (spec/std-lib/prof.md §6).

   Each program imports the bundled module via [?lib 'cx-stdlib/prof']
   (resolver MUST be quoted) then calls [$prof:fn args]. The LAST
   expression is the rendered result. String results render double-quoted
   by the runner; null as `null`; int bare; bool bare; elements as
   `[head attr=… …]`; maps in their canonical render.

   CAPABILITY MODEL (spec §7). The stdlib eval runner evaluates each case
   under code.new_env(), which grants NO capabilities (deny-by-default
   per spec/core/security.md §2). prof splits cleanly:

     - clock-gated (§7): now-ns, now-cpu-ns, time-fn, time-and-trace,
       trace. The effect point checks the active set FIRST; with the
       `clock` grant absent these raise E_CAP_DENIED = cx-err:CXER0271
       (security.md §4). That capability-denied error is therefore the
       deterministic, spec-faithful happy-path assertion for every
       clock-gated function — real clock reads are non-reproducible and
       AUTHORING.md ("Capability-gated / effectful") directs us to
       fixture the capability-denied error rather than real effects.

     - no-capability (§7): counter-*, histogram-*, mem-snapshot,
       gc-trigger, flamegraph-emit, trace-flush, prof-configure. These
       run in-process and produce real values under the empty grant set,
       so their behavior is asserted directly.

   CXER2102 (time-fn non-callable thunk) is NOT asserted here. time-fn is
   clock-gated; under the empty grant set the capability check
   short-circuits FIRST, so a correct impl raises CXER0271 — not
   CXER2102 — for a no-cap call regardless of thunk callability.
   Asserting CXER2102 would FAIL a correct impl (AUTHORING.md
   "Grounding": a wrong fixture is worse than a missing one). Its
   scenario belongs in a capability-granted harness (conformance/stdlib/prof.cxd)
   once the host-grant fixture surface exists. See the report's
   `ambiguous` list.

   DETERMINISM: counter / histogram / trace state is process-global
   (§3.3/§3.4); each case composes its own setup via [?let] and reads
   back a stable derived value (a counter total, a count, a null return)
   rather than a volatile measurement. mem-snapshot's field values
   (rss-bytes, timestamp, …) are inherently volatile (§2.2), so its happy
   path asserts only the stable invariant the spec guarantees — the
   required `rss-bytes` field is present and positive — via a comparison
   that renders the deterministic `true`.

   NOTE: no inter-fixture comment blocks appear between cases — a comment
   between sections risks capture into the preceding section payload; all
   explanatory prose lives in this doc header or in tags.
#]]

 [case id=prof-001-now-ns-cap-denied level=core
  [tags prof now-ns clock capability CXER0271]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/prof']
[$prof:now-ns]
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0271
#]]
 ]

 [case id=prof-002-now-cpu-ns-cap-denied level=core
  [tags prof now-cpu-ns clock capability CXER0271]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/prof']
[$prof:now-cpu-ns]
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0271
#]]
 ]

